## Know the warning signs

Your asthma may be getting worse if you:

- use your reliever **more often** than usual
- wake up at night with **coughing or wheezing**
- find it harder to do **everyday things**
- have a **lower peak flow** than usual

Follow your action plan, and see your GP or asthma nurse soon.

## Avoiding triggers

- **Pollen:** check the pollen forecast, keep windows closed on high pollen days, and shower after being outside
- **Dust mites:** wash bedding weekly at 60°C, and vacuum regularly
- **Mould:** keep rooms aired and deal with damp
- **Pollution:** check air quality forecasts and avoid busy roads when you can
- **Colds:** wash your hands often and keep up with vaccines

## Exercise

Being active is good for your lungs. Warm up first, keep your reliever with you, and talk to your nurse if exercise often sets off your asthma.

## Smoking and vaping

**Stopping smoking**, and avoiding other people's smoke, is one of the best things you can do. Your local stop smoking service can help, for free.

## Travel and holidays

Take enough medicine for your trip, plus extra. Keep your inhaler in your hand luggage, and take a copy of your action plan.

## Support

**Asthma and Lung UK** runs a free helpline with respiratory nurse specialists.
